> -          If it is illogical for any reason to apply the mirt function in the manner described above, given the nature of my dataset

Seems fine to me. Though I think the discussion you linked to was more with respect to different groups of individuals who answered at least some common items, and the question becomes whether there are any behavioural differences that way. What you described sounds more like a single group case with NAs are used as placeholders for items not responded to, which is perfectly fine.

>
> -          If there are any specific checks before/after applying the mirt function that I need to do, given the nature of my data, beyond the following basics: trace and test info plots; Yen’s Q3; summary(expmodel); and, coef(expmodel, IRTpars = T)

I don't believe so. Checking overall model fit via M2() might be helpful, as well as the general itemfit()s to see if the IRT models you fitted provide evidence of reasonable goodness-of-fit. HTH.

Phil

